{"entries":[{"id":"tsk-luk-9-1","title":"Luke 9:1","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"LUK","chapterStart":9,"verseStart":1,"chapterEnd":9,"verseEnd":1},"content":"<p>1 Christ sends his apostles to work miracles, and to preach. 7 Herod desires to see Christ. 10 The apostles return. 12 Christ feeds five thousand; 18 enquires what opinion the world had of him; foretells his passion; 23 proposes to all the pattern of his patience. 28 The transfiguration. 37 He heals the lunatic; 43 again forewarns his disciples of his passion; 46 commends humility; 51 bids them to shew mildness towards all, without desire of revenge. 57 Divers would follow him, but upon conditions.</p>\n<p>he. 6:13-16; Mt 10:2-5; Mr 3:13-19; 6:7-13 gave. 10:19; Mt 10:1; 16:19; Mr 6:7; 16:17,18; Joh 14:12; Ac 1:8; 3:16 Ac 4:30; 9:34</p>"},{"id":"tsk-luk-9-2","title":"Luke 9:2","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"LUK","chapterStart":9,"verseStart":2,"chapterEnd":9,"verseEnd":2},"content":"<p>10:1,9,11; 16:16; Mt 3:2; 10:7,8; 13:19; 24:14; Mr 1:14,15; 6:12 Mr 16:15; Heb 2:3,4</p>"},{"id":"tsk-luk-9-3","title":"Luke 9:3","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"LUK","chapterStart":9,"verseStart":3,"chapterEnd":9,"verseEnd":3},"content":"<p>Take. 10:4-12; 12:22; 22:35; Ps 37:3; Mt 10:9,10; Mr 6:8,9; 2Ti 2:4 two. 3:11; 5:29; 12:28</p>"},{"id":"tsk-luk-9-4","title":"Luke 9:4","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"LUK","chapterStart":9,"verseStart":4,"chapterEnd":9,"verseEnd":4},"content":"<p>10:5-8; Mt 10:11; Mr 6:10; Ac 16:15</p>"},{"id":"tsk-luk-9-5","title":"Luke 9:5","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"LUK","chapterStart":9,"verseStart":5,"chapterEnd":9,"verseEnd":5},"content":"<p>whosoever. 48; 10:10-12,16; Mt 10:14,15; Mr 6:11; 9:37; Ac 13:51; 18:6 shake. 53-56; Ne 5:13 a testimony. 5:14; Mt 10:18</p>"},{"id":"tsk-luk-9-6","title":"Luke 9:6","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"LUK","chapterStart":9,"verseStart":6,"chapterEnd":9,"verseEnd":6},"content":"<p>1,2; Mr 6:12,13; 16:20; Ac 4:30; 5:15</p>"},{"id":"tsk-luk-9-7","title":"Luke 9:7","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"LUK","chapterStart":9,"verseStart":7,"chapterEnd":9,"verseEnd":7},"content":"<p>A.M. 4036. A.D. 32. Herod. Job 18:11,12; Ps 73:19; Mt 14:1-12; Mr 6:14-28 Tetrarch.A tetrarch, [tetrarches ,] from [tetras ,] four, and [arche ,] government, properly signifies a prince, or ruler over a quarter of any region; and had its origin from Galatia, which was governed by four princes. In the New Testament, however, it denotes a prince, or king, who reigns over the fourth part of a former kingdom. By Herod's will his kingdom was thus divided among his sons: Archelaus had one-half, consisting of Idumea, Judea, and Samaria; Herod Antipas, one-fourth, consisting of Galilee and Perea; and Philip the remaining fourth, consisting of Batanea, Trachonitis, and Auranitis.</p>\n<p>he. 21:25; Isa 22:5; Mic 7:4</p>"},{"id":"tsk-luk-9-8","title":"Luke 9:8","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"LUK","chapterStart":9,"verseStart":8,"chapterEnd":9,"verseEnd":8},"content":"<p>19; Mt 17:10; Mr 6:15; 8:28; Joh 1:21</p>"},{"id":"tsk-luk-9-9","title":"Luke 9:9","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"LUK","chapterStart":9,"verseStart":9,"chapterEnd":9,"verseEnd":9},"content":"<p>John. 7 And he. 13:31,32; 23:8</p>"},{"id":"tsk-luk-9-10","title":"Luke 9:10","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"LUK","chapterStart":9,"verseStart":10,"chapterEnd":9,"verseEnd":10},"content":"<p>the apostles. 10:17; Zec 1:10; Mr 6:30; Heb 13:17 he took. Mt 14:13; Mr 2:7; 6:31,32 Bethsaida. Mt 11:21; Mr 6:45; Joh 1:44</p>"},{"id":"tsk-luk-9-11","title":"Luke 9:11","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"LUK","chapterStart":9,"verseStart":11,"chapterEnd":9,"verseEnd":11},"content":"<p>when. Mt 14:14; Mr 6:33,34; Ro 10:14,17 and he. Isa 61:1; Joh 4:34; 6:37; Ro 15:3; 2Ti 4:2 the k